    {
      "id": "84dd15ef-5e39-487c-b9e9-c120828aebf7",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Findings\n\n**Does possession help you win? Yes — but much less than the cliché\nsuggests, and mostly by proxy.**\n\n1. **The relationship is real but modest.** In the Premier League, a\n   team's win rate climbs from 26.6% with under 35% possession to\n   54.1% above 70% — a near-doubling, but a >70% possessor still drops\n   points nearly a quarter of the time. Across all covered leagues the\n   curve is flatter still (31.7% → 48.0%).\n\n2. **Possession is a team-quality marker more than a match-winner.**\n   Correlating each team's *season-average* possession with its final\n   points gives ρ = 0.704 across 431 EPL team-seasons — possession tells\n   you who's good. But within a single match, possession correlates\n   with winning at only ρ = 0.119, while **shot share correlates at\n   0.258** — shots predict victory more than twice as well. On the\n   small xG-covered subset, xG share reaches 0.438.\n\n3. **The extremes cut both ways.** 12.7% of EPL wins were earned with\n   under 40% possession; the same share of losses came with over 60%.\n   Nottingham Forest won 63% of their matches despite low possession;\n   Chelsea lost 43% of theirs *while dominating the ball*.\n\n4. **Context matters.** Possession is nearly worthless against weak\n   opponents (win rate ~45-58% across all bands — anyone can have the\n   ball vs a bad team) but separates winners from losers against strong\n   ones (22% → 40%). Away from home, even >70% possession wins only\n   47.3% of the time versus 58.7% at home.\n\n5. **No historical trend.** The yearly correlation between possession\n   differential and home wins fluctuates between ~0.02 and ~0.28 with\n   no directional movement — possession has not become more (or less)\n   decisive across two decades.\n\n**Verdict:** possession is football's most quoted stat and one of its\nweakest match-level predictors. It measures *who you are* far better\nthan *what will happen today* — and when it comes to predicting a\nsingle result, shots and xG do the job at roughly 2–4× the strength.",
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": []
    }
